									June 1998
AĞILS

	AHMET BAŞAR
	ÇEKİRDEKSİZ- 
		Haci Şaban,  960m.; 
		Haci Mehmet, 910m.
		Orta Ağıl, 	860m.

	
We started a survey of sheepfolds (SF) in the Kızıllı mevki of the village of Çekirdeksiz (this location is where the first migration from early settlers of people from “Kışla” mevki near Kızlarkayası,YH -- due to mosquitos, in 1890s). Most of the Sheep Folds (SF) were within a distance of 5.7-8.2 km. from ÇK.
So far, 8 SF were visited, an area of about 32 km2, including the farmed flat plains and hills and ridges beneath Medetkaşı Tepe (1214 m.) The SF are located at elev. ranging from 830-960m.from the plain.
Volcanic area; earth has shifted to form deep gulleys. Stream and a natural water source nearby. Characterized by numerous hills with both steep and gentle slopes. 
Generally located on more gentle slopes, sometimes built into a slope or resting against a natural rock base...protected from northerly winds.

14.6.98

At Çekirdeksiz – with Orhan Akkaya.
Drove north, c. 5 km. passed through Künk Deresi which is flowing through a deep gorge. Here there are 8 SF: Erkeç, Muhtar’ın, Hacı Boşnak, Orta Yatak, Ahmet Çavuş, Çolak Ismail, Recep Şahin (Söğütlerin yanı), Atibey (Gölün yanı)..In the past, there were no outsiders from Bolu renting SF, because we had a much larger flock. But now we rent out some of them.
Farmland is expanding from the flat plains into the more gentle slopes..e.g. Orta Ağıl, Izzet Ağa (both dismantled); latter one has fields of flax, sunflower, melons, plus wheat, barley, oats..Others located near ridges will not be used for farming, until they are ruined by nature or man..
These folds are close to one another, generally separated by a ridge--e.g. cave SF and Recep’in ağılı; they are separated by a SF are called by names of their owners, mostly. ridge-barrier. This may also tell us that each group utilized the grazing land selectively, in order not to interfere with each others’ herds..ie. conserving grazing land. 
(Medetkaşı altı mevki; - Şabanözü mevkiin arka kısmı; Çolak Ismail; mevki)
There used to be about 12 ağıls, until about 1965. Then it started decreasing. 
SF location is selected to protect animals from winter climate and also to give thm as much sun as possible. So, they face south, early morning sun.
SF is surrounded by a wall or fence; in winter they are sheltered under the roofed area of the SF. We don’t have conflicts with farmers, we are all from the same village.

Royal Road - starts from near the base of MM- it passes through Belkavak, Çimenceğiz and go to Ankara.. that is the ancient Road.

15.6.98
With old farmer, Ahmet Başar, cousin of “Selektör Hasan Özmen”, from Çekirdeksiz. 
In front of a large cave, surrounded by a stone wall. Cave accomodates c. 300-400 goats. In total, c. 1,000. This is a very large plain below, we can see as far as YH.
In 1930’s this wide plain was all for grazing. Bolu shepherds were coming to graze, also. In early 1940, Benli İsmail bought Kızıllı plain from an owner from Beypazarı (Seferoğlu--He was Yurdagül’s great great uncle, from Sazılar). And, since 1942, it is being farmed. 
Shepherds were coming to graze, also, brought at most 2,000 animals. Stayed till early May. 
In winter time we don’t leave our animals in ağıls.  Only in summer,  YOZ comes.
From Aug. to Nov. milking ones join them.

Now we have altogether 4,000. It used to be 17,000 about 50 years ago.We only use Recep’in agılı for the village flock (YOZ).
It is not profitable to keep sheep anymore - I have 80 sheep, 3 cows. The milk that they produce would be equivalent in price to  15 tons of barley..,
Fold locations are selected near a spring, for ex. SF (AZ1) ,It is built of stone, shrubs/reeds.  In summer the herd stays largely outdoors, but within the fenced area, sheepdogs on the periphery, with one eye open at all times.

15.9.98

Going with Ahmet Başar to SF. At Cave (AZ1). 
It is covered with Kerme. A very large area.. which accomodates over 1,000 animals. But it is difficult to transport it, so they leave it here. These SF have become “transitional”places, at best 2-3 months duration. But some are rented by outsiders, for ex. Bolu shepherds, they use it for 
6 1/2 months, starting right after the village herds leave. This is the case with AZ1.
 
SALT: In the past we gave them rock salt. Now, we give them powdered salt.
We put them on stones, it is good for growth, to eat well, makes them drink.. given once a week. 
Salt was kept either in shepherd’s hut or in another storage. Some kept it in pits outdoors, near the hut. Also, barley, flour were stored during winter.
He made his and dogs’ food himself. He stayed outdoors by the sheep sometimes, wearing his “kepenek”. 
Sheep tend to get “kene” on their feet..it may be fatal to them, shepherd has to be very careful about checking them and himself. Apple and ash from kemre are traditional medicines . Now they use DDT (hazardous)!  
Lice also eats their ears.

Erkeç (MK2) - It is also called Yukarı ağıl. Surrounded by a reed wall. Nearby there are fields of reeds growing in the flowing stream, (photographed). It is below a ridge, very uneven surface..different parts of SF built to fit into the landform; e.g. open part is on a slope, whereas the roofed areas are on flatland.
Now this SF is rented to people from Kıbriscık.
They also had separate fold for male goats: Erkek keçi yatağı. 
Shepherds from Kıbrıscık..they walk both ways, stay here 6 1/2 months. Walking is not very easy, because one has to negotiate with villagers along the way. They all want a lamb in return for food and passage..you wouldn’t have lambs left in the herd if you gave each what he asked. 
In the past especially the goat herders chose to pass by mountains and collect whatever they could along the way, so as not to give in to the villagers. Of course there was much more grazing in the past. 
Near Recep’in ağılı there is a dismantled one, Orta Ağıl. There is nothing there, except for a change in vegetation and color. May be a few stones left.
The owner moved to Polatlı, his children sold the animals and possibly part of the land in the adjoining areas. 
(Later, we went to see it, yes, there is nothing left, except for the remains of a rather substantial stone wall, on a slight rise, overlooking the fold across the open fold).
Ahmet guessed it might have been the shepherd’s hut).

On the way to Orta Ağıl we came across a type of thorny plant with a large bulbous flower, thorns radiating from it. He called in “kenger”. It is an edible plant, after removing the thorns. It is prepared like spinach. It has a kind of tmilk in the stalk, they make sakız from it. Geven Dikeni - when there was not enough hay, we gave them this as food...
KIRKMAK - Around first week in June we shear “yoz”; a month later we do “sa€mal”. Because yoz begins to sweat which makes it easier to shear. Others are a bit weak, they’ve been milking..they sweat later. “Eski yün deriden kolay ayrılıyor”.
In 1927 there was a big drought here. That was when the owner of Kızıllı farm went bankrupt and he sold it to Çekirdeksiz. Now 48 families have shares in it.
, originating from Medetkaﬂı. We also brought our cattle to a€ıl. Now we have much fewer animals, almost no goats.NOur herds walk to a€ıl for c. 5-6 km. 

SF Mevkileri: Belkavak ;  Medetkaşı (Hacı Boşnak, Erkeç, Muhtarın yatağı); Şabanözü mevki arkası (Ahmet Çavuşun, Recep ﬁahin’in yatağı); Gölün yanı (Atibey)
 Background can be against a living rock, this way there is both protection from wild animals and cold winds and less wall area to enclose., Midas Tumulus is seen in profile through the heat haze. This SF used to be only for he-goats; its counterpart for she-goats is the Cave fold, which is c. 5 km. distance over the ridge, to the east. They were separated because at this season many she-goats were pregnant; the he-goats would go long distances, forcing the weak she-goats to come after them, and that would not be good.

6-27
We built a fountain near Recep’in ağılı, collecting water from springs from Medetkaşı for our animals--planted willows near it for the shepherds and us.
Kızıllı is a very wide and large plain, bordering villages of YH, ŞÖ, Basrı (15,000 ha).  
At ÇK arming began around 1942. 
In 1940’s there were c. 17,000 animals. Now, c. 3,000. Even outsider shepherds brought 1,500 or more animals here. 

17.6.98
With Ahmet Dede. Şabanözü hududu içindeyiz. Çevrede tarlalar var; a lot of sun flowers, watermelon, melon farmed. “Kepir” toprak; killi toprak. If it is very white, it would not be fertile..still a lot of wild barley, poppies and wild flowers, “kısa Mahmut” with yellow flowers with sweet fragrance.
Volcanic hills. According to Lisa and Arlene, here there is a large 1st mill. settlement. Phrygian ceramics.
We  are going to the Izzet ağıl. It is no longer in use, since 1965.It used to be a year-round ağıl.
Nothing is left..only a square area in brown color. Behind is a slight rise. Up the rise is a small, protected area where they kept the lambs, so they wouldn’t nurse anymore. 
There is a bunch of willows which usually indicates the presence of water nearby.
Also, one wild pear tree “alıç”..probably there used to be many more.
Now, on both sides of the a€ıl there are hemp fields (keten)..it is a beautiful, greenish, shocking yellow. He had 1,000 s &g.
Sheep slept in open; goats were inside the fold. After they were sheared (early March) they would catch cold..that’s why they stayed indoors. 
Four men would shear 100 goats/day..
 After it was dismantled, the grazing land around it was sold out, and it began to be farmed..


Recep Doruk - SF are built facing “kıble”, toward southeast, so the animals get the benefit of nature from sun. Animals drink in summer 2x/day, not more. Our fathers knew where to build them: mild, sunny climate, water for men and animals, not far from village, protected from predators..
	Merinos vs. Karaman species - Karaman has tasty meat, its wool is good for bedding; Merinos is fatter and thus more meat, and gives birth twice and to twins.
We get lambs twice a year; and if it has twins, you get 4/year. So, that’s very profitable. I am born into a shepherd family. I am from Yazıca. 

27.6.98
Walking with Ömer Şimşek at YH - from east of MM

 toward south. Mercan, his dog and ass are walking with us.
Pastureland Boundaries - to the east is Kızıllı to Şabanözü village; Upper Sakarya Plain, near YH. We are investigating a triangular area,  with ŞÖ on the northern tip, from Basrı to Duatepe (Çekirdeksiz) as the base, to the  village of Beyceğiz and to the west Nohutluk and Kabayazı düzü.. an area of 10x15 km., not including pastureland of YH.

Ömer Şimşek: When I came here in 1940’s I became a shepherd to all these villages, and herding was extremely important, definitely farming was secondary to it.
If a man had 500-1,000 animals, and if he only had 100 d. of land. It was done with horse and bull..He would hire several shepherds..In 1952 with Land reform, pastoral activities started decreasing (also, adoption of tractor)..

An observation: As we were walking by the fields across the street from Midas Mound, I noticed that Ömer and Mercan managed to keep the herd in line parallel to the fields rather than letting them into it. Ömer was walking next to the fields, Mercan to his left, and the herds to Mercan’s left. Herds were shielded from the newly-ripened wheat and barley. A good strategy!

I came to Polatlı in 1947. Pastoral life was much more prevalent. I worked at Kıranharman Çiftlik, Beyliköprü, iğciler, Eski Polatlı, Şabanözü, Çayören’de çalıştım. For ex. in ŞÖ one person owned 1,000 animals, and there were about 10 herds. I took them to Medet, Çile, Güney..belong to ŞÖ. At KH, there were about 800/herd. Grazed them at Çallıbük, east Sakarya valley.. At BK, c. 1000/herd.
Mama deresi environs. At ÇK, grazing at Medet and Dua Tepe.

At Iğciler, there is a region called Botçalıöz, and that’s where they grazed their animals. (Botça= Şarbon hastalığı)

At Sarıkaya - the SF there has been around for at least 50 years.

Ayşe: I noticed that the sheep were eating fresh clover-like grasses in small puddles of water; there is also oregano and other smelly grasses. Ömer said that when animals graze, land rejuvenates itself. But beware of the tractor and fire..those will kill all natural vegetation. (Naomi said that there ara clover-like grasses of several genuses, called Trigonella which animals love. That’s what they were eating today in small puddles). They are clever!
From near Midas we can see Beştepeler at Iğciler. That’s a place with SF..
Selection of Ağıl Placement - (Recai Doruk) 
Herds stay in ağıl mostly in winter; they don’t sleep in during summer. So, they usually face southeast, to keep warm, in winter..it is like all animals. Bees also make their nest facing SE. 
Near a stream for shepherd and herds. 

Sheep and goats (caprids) don’t drink water all the time..only twice a day, in summer.  Otherwise, they would get bloated, what with regurgitating all the time.

Up until 40 years ago we had exclusively Karaman; now, it is mainly Merinos.
Karaman is good for meat and wool for bedding; Merinos yields more lambs.
With more lambs, in the short run, you profit from sale of lambs.

Kızıllı Çiftliği and environs,. 150,000 dönüms; ie. 15,000 ha. 

ŞABANÖZÜ - History of Herding (c. 40-50 years ago) –
Saffet Üstün 
“Agriculture was way behind herding. With horse and bull; no chemical fertilizer, no tractor..we used dung as fuel, not sufficient for fertilizer.  
Had lots of herds..Izzet ağa had 3 ağıls; he had 600-700 sheep and more than 1,000 goats. Osman Bağcı etc...village had more than 10,000..
Now, at most 4,000..
We used goat for “kurban” (male); tiftik was cheap, sold it to tiftik cooperative in Polatlı..I sold my goats to people from Manisa. 

The SF are located at elev. ranging from 830-960m. from the plain. Generally located on more gentle slopes, sometimes built into a slope or resting against a natural rock base...protected from northerly winds; 
also on hills and ridges beneath Medetkaşı Tepe, Volcanic area at (1214 m.). 
The earth has shifted to form deep gulleys. Stream and a natural water source nearby. Characterized by numerous hills with both steep and gentle slopes. 



